#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162974} :activity/id #uuid "9110c851-c32a-57df-a77a-329797a3df41" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048731 :topic/id #uuid "b99b224f-8118-53d5-8d6e-c47785040b57" :topic/math-academy-id 3028} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162056 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161856 :question/id #uuid "01fc4e2e-d93f-50b3-859e-19732d4f10c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9111"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "405984cd-1e1b-54c6-bc75-268e127b9812" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050327}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420139}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Constant"} :step/id #uuid "3174d04a-7e8e-5098-91d3-c55afedd2e39" :step/math-academy-id 33959 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162975}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162972 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162057 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161857 :question/id #uuid "a3f88c0e-9426-50d2-baf0-95909e222d4b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9112"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a3752a0-431d-5520-97b1-493c36bbce54" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420140}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Scaled Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "fe3d0b73-cb7e-5370-9455-c490da334890" :step/math-academy-id 33961 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162976}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162058 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161858 :question/id #uuid "c20c7b15-e967-52a9-846d-4915f6661158" :question/math-academy-id "e-9114"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85388f7d-b2ee-520b-b711-a7721c6f620c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420138}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Transformed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "09f12104-7949-5867-9884-48e422a32857" :step/math-academy-id 33963 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162977}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163273 :tutorial/content "There are several important properties of the variance of a random variable that we can use to simplify expressions. \n\nFirstly, the variance of a constant is always zero. That is, for any constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[a] = 0.\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. A constant does not vary, so the mean deviation from its expected value should be zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "6eb17ec7-1e57-5d75-8a71-9030a420602b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5458 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "65bbda75-37ec-5809-926a-0fb8d31c008d" :step/math-academy-id 33964 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162971}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162975 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163274 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ is a constant. Then,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nSo, for example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-4X] & =(-4)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To understand why we have to square the constant when we factor it outside the variance, it's helpful to remember that the variance is defined as the expected *squared* deviation from the mean:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ]\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX] & =\\text{E}[(aX-\\text{E}[aX])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d33b6982-066e-5c82-8138-0283ad8dccbb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5459 :tutorial/title "The Variance of a Scaled Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "55f9fd86-1f4a-5f2c-b7c2-a60113a4a9e8" :step/math-academy-id 33966 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162972}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163275 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ and $b$ are constants. It can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nA proof of this result is given at the end of this lesson.\n\nTherefore, when a random variable is scaled by a factor of $a$ and shifted by a constant $b\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- the variance is scaled by a factor of $a^2$ as before, and\n\n- the variance is unaffected by the shift.\n\nThe fact that shifting a random variable leaves the variance unaltered makes intuitive sense. Shifting all values of $X$ by a fixed amount does not change how far apart those values are from each other. \n\nFor example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X+1] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1523e51b-84da-5100-809f-09fbcb0d80ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5460 :tutorial/title "The Variance of a Transformed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "e17beec2-91ed-5375-9c58-ab279784f2bf" :step/math-academy-id 33968 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162973}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163276 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X]\n$$\n\nRecall that, by definition,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX+b] & =\\text{E}[(aX+b-\\text{E}[aX+b])^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expected value, we can simplify this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[(aX+b-\\text{E}[aX+b])^{2}] & =\\text{E}[(aX+b-a\\text{E}[X]-b)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(a(X-\\text{E}[X]))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0372f0de-b6a6-58ee-8195-c04024e8f745" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5461 :tutorial/title "Proof of the General Formula"} :step/id #uuid "da23e59c-3020-5db2-8f5b-77675938bf0b" :step/math-academy-id 33969}] :activity/title "Properties of Variance for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162181}]}
